**PURPOSE OF JOB CLASS (NATURE OF WORK)**
-----------------------------------------


In the Department of Energy and Environmental Protection this class is accountable for directing the staff and programs of a scientific, regulatory and/or technical division.
**EXAMPLES OF DUTIES**
----------------------


* Directs staff and operations of a scientific, regulatory and/or technical division;
* Coordinates, plans and manages division activities;
* Formulates and ensures implementation of program goals, objectives, new procedures and procedural revisions;
* Develops, implements and evaluates division policies, goals and objectives;
* Administers state and federal laws and regulations pertaining to division;
* Designs and implements performance review standards for division staff;
* Prepares or assists in preparation of division budget;
* Maintains contacts and liaison with public and private individuals and organizations and other agency units with potential to impact program activities;
* Recommends appropriate staffing levels;
* Develops new laws and regulations related to division programs;
* Serves as state liaison with federal programs;
* Leads the recruitment and hiring of staff, including outreach, interview and selection;
* Performs related duties as required.
**KNOWLEDGE, SKILL AND ABILITY**
--------------------------------


* Considerable knowledge of
	+ and ability to apply management principles and practices;
	+ relevant state and federal laws, statutes and regulations;
	+ and ability to apply relevant agency policies and procedures;
	+ energy, environmental or radiation protection principles, techniques, practices and issues;
* Considerable
	+ interpersonal skills;
	+ oral and written communication skills;
	+ analytical, organizational and negotiation skills.
**MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS \\- GENERAL EXPERIENCE**
------------------------------------------------


Ten (10\\) years of professional experience in natural resource management, public outdoor recreation, environmental education, conservation law enforcement, pollution prevention, reduction and management, radiation control, radiological regulatory programs or integration of radiological policies and programs, environmental or energy conservation, environmental or energy regulatory programs, integration of energy and environmental policies and programs, energy research or policy development, strategic operational environmental planning, energy planning, radiological planning, emergency response or other related environmental protection or energy field.
**MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS \\- SPECIAL EXPERIENCE**
------------------------------------------------


Two (2\\) years of the General Experience must have been in a supervisory capacity responsible for the supervision of professional and technical support staff performing energy or environmental protection work,  

OR  

One (1\\) year of the General Experience must have been in a managerial capacity responsible for the management of professional and technical support staff performing energy or environmental protection work or accountable for establishing and monitoring energy and environmental financial and/or business policies and/or procedures.  



NOTE:
* Supervisory capacity is defined as scheduling, assigning and overseeing work, establishing performance standards for employees and taking corrective measures to implement those standards.
* For state employees supervisory experience is interpreted at the level of a Supervising Environmental Engineer (Environmental Protection), Environmental Protection Supervising Air Pollution Control Engineer, Environmental Protection Supervising Radiation Control Physicist, Environmental Protection Supervising Civil Engineer, Supervising Environmental Analyst, Environmental Protection Supervising Forester, Environmental Protection Supervising Fisheries Biologist, Environmental Protection Supervising Wildlife Biologist, Public Utilities Supervisor of Technical Analysis or other comparable supervisory title responsible for the supervision of professional and technical support staff performing energy or environmental protection work.
* Managerial capacity is defined as formulating program goals and objectives, developing and implementing program procedures, initiating program policies and developing and monitoring a budget.
* For state employees managerial experience is interpreted at the level of an Energy and Environmental Protection Assistant Division Director, Energy and Environmental Protection Office Director or other comparable managerial title responsible for the management of professional and technical support staff performing energy or environmental protection work.
**MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS \\- SUBSTITUTIONS ALLOWED**
---------------------------------------------------


* College training in a scientific or technical discipline related to the energy or environmental field including but not limited to the biological, earth or physical sciences, health physics, physics, nuclear engineering or radiation science, environmental planning, conservation law enforcement, environmental law, economics, leisure services, geographic information systems, geography with a concentration in geographic information systems, natural sciences, natural resources, renewable energy, energy conservation or a related field may be substituted for the General Experience on the basis of fifteen (15\\) semester hours equalling one\\-half (1/2\\) year of experience to a maximum of four (4\\) years for a Bachelor's degree.
* A Master's degree in a scientific or technical discipline related to the energy or environmental field including but not limited to the biological, earth or physical sciences, health physics, physics, nuclear engineering or radiation science, environmental planning, conservation law enforcement, environmental law, economics, leisure services, geographic information systems, geography with a concentration in geographic information systems, natural sciences, natural resources, renewable energy, energy conservation or a related field may be substituted for five (5\\) years of the General Experience.
* A law degree with a concentration in energy, natural resources or environmental protection law or a doctorate degree in the biological, earth or physical sciences, health physics, physics, nuclear engineering or radiation science or a field related to renewable energy, energy conservation or environmental protection may be substituted for six (6\\) years of the General Experience.
NOTE: Education may only be substituted for a total of six (6\\) years of the General Experience.  

**PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS**
----------------------------


* Experience managing and completing projects, including making decisions on project approach, setting measurable goals and timelines for project completion and delivering project outcomes;
* Experience managing two or more work tasks or projects simultaneously and prioritizing to achieve leadership’s goals;
* Experience collaborating with colleagues and managing conflict to lead cross\\-functional teams engaged in technical and policy work;
* Experience applying policies and procedures to lead or manage employees;
* Experience collaborating with state and federal agencies, environmental advocacy groups, legislators, and regional organizations;
* Experience in environmental protection and management, including experience in materials management;
* Experience administering grant or financial assistance programs;
* Experience administering regulatory programs;
* Experience formulating program or organization goals and objectives;
* Experience initiating and developing policies and developing policy reports.
**SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS**
------------------------


* Incumbents in this class will be required to possess and retain a valid Motor Vehicle Operator’s license.
* Incumbents in this class may be required to travel.
* Incumbents in this class may be required to obtain a security clearance.
* Incumbents in this class may be required by the appointing authority to possess and retain appropriate current licenses, permits and/or certifications.
Radiation Division:
* Incumbents in this class may be required to respond to radiological emergencies within Connecticut and other New England states.

**JOB DESCRIPTION**

Dominion Water \\& Sanitation District (“the District”) is a special district formed in 2004 to provide wholesale water and wastewater services. The District was formed as part of the County’s vision to bring renewable water and access to centralized wastewater services to Northwest Douglas County. The district is building an integrated system that fully and efficiently utilizes its resources. By strategic partnering, planning for both long and short term, and integrating technology, the district is developing a reliable system for cost\\-effective service.

To support the District as it continues to grow the District is seeking a Director of Finance and Accounting who excels in a fast\\-paced dynamic environment with both internal and external stakeholders. Under direction of the General Manager (the “GM”), the Director of Finance and Accounting will be responsible for overseeing the finance and accounting operations of Dominion Water \\& Sanitation District (“Dominion”). Your areas of responsibilities will include:

* Cash management
* Accounting
* Financial statements
* Reporting
* Budget preparation and planning
* Short\\-term and long\\-term financial forecasts
* Capital asset planning
* Debt management
* Audit
* Rate studies

We are looking for a candidate with a strong foundation in finance, accounting and business operations with the ability to analyze financial statements trends, develop and maintain complex financial forecasting models, and communicate effectively with members of Senior Management, the Board of Directors (the “Board) and other stakeholders.

This position requires an energetic, decisive, and detail\\-oriented person. The ideal candidate is an organized, driven individual with excellent communication skills who can analyze, assess, and solve problems.

The Director of Finance and Accounting will work closely with Senior Management, the Board of Directors, and other stakeholders to:

* Oversee the finance and accounting operations of Dominion;
* Supervise finance and accounting staff in recording Dominion’s business activities including cash receipts and disbursements, accounts payable and receivable, debt service, capital costs, draw requests and journal entries necessary to prepare financial statements;
* Coordinate and oversee the preparation of financial statements and other reports with Dominion’s outside accountants for the Board and Finance Committee as well as other required report disclosures;
* Coordinate and oversee the annual financial audit;
* Work with other members of Senior Management to analyze Dominion’s operations, agreements and commitments and recommend and implement solutions relating to managing Dominion’s operations, finances, and long\\-term business plans;
* Ensure all Dominion funds are properly expended in the best interests of Dominion and its customers;
* Coordinate the preparation of reimbursement and draw requests to fund expenditures of Dominion’s capital projects;
* Develop and maintain a short\\-term (3\\-5 years) and long\\-term financial forecast model, including a capital improvement plan and strategy to support a growing customer base;
* Support the execution of short\\-term and long\\-term financial plans;
* Oversee the annual budget process and coordinate with Senior Management and Dominion’s outside accountants to prepare, present, monitor and adjust annual budgets for approval by the Board;
* Work with Dominion’s Rate Study consultants to review Dominion’s fees and fee structures and recommend modifications based upon study findings;
* Develop strategies for identifying sources of revenue and financing opportunities;
* Work with other members of Senior Management to research, identify and secure grant and other public funding opportunities benefiting Dominion;
* Monitor compliance with debt covenants;
* Develop strategies to optimize the utilization of Dominion’s assets;
* Coordinate presentation materials for Board meetings and external communications;
* Effectively build formal and informal relationship networks inside and outside the organization;

**Qualifications**

* Strong data and analytics skills;
* Fluent in Microsoft Excel, Word, and Quickbooks as well as Adobe;
* Solid managerial and interpersonal skills;
* Proven track record of effectively interacting with senior management;
* Ability to effectively supervise staff on finance and accounting matters;
* Ability to work strategically and collaboratively;
* Ability to think strategically and creatively;
* Proven experience with financial management, decision\\-making, and management of major capital investment programs and evaluating operating costs;
* Experience with short\\-term and long\\-term financial planning for a special district or municipality preferred;
* Proven ability for leading and delivering internal projects;
* Expert problem solver. Leverages knowledge gained through prior experience, education, or training to resolve issues and remove project barriers;
* Fosters a climate conducive to establishing positive working relationships both within and outside of an organization;
* Excellent written and verbal communications skills required. Must be able to easily grasp and communicate complex ideas.

**Education And Experience**

* Minimum 7 years of relevant finance and operations experience required with strong business, financial and/or accounting experience; Water and Sewer Utility operations, finance and accounting experience preferred.
* Bachelor's Degree in Business, Finance, Accounting, or related field, MBA or CPA preferred.
* Minimum of 3 years of supervisory experience
* Formal leadership and business administration training desired.

This position performs other related duties and functions as may be delegated, assigned, or required. The statements contained herein reflect general details as necessary to describe the primary and delegated responsibilities of the job as Director of Finance and Accounting but are not an all\\-inclusive listing of work requirements. The Director of Finance and Accounting may be required to perform other tasks as deemed necessary to accomplish the job which is a leadership responsibility that grows and evolves with the organization.

Submit Resume and Cover Letter to info@dominionwsd.com

Pay: $165,000\\.00 \\- $180,000\\.00 per year

Benefits:

* 401(k)
* 401(k) matching
* Dental insurance
* Health insurance
* Life insurance
* Paid time off
* Vision insurance

Work Location: In person

**Department:** Philanthropy Group  



**Reports To:** Executive Director, Haiti Development Institute  



**FLSA Classification:** Exempt **FTE:** 1  



**Supervises:** 1 (Occasional Interns and Consultants)  



**Hybrid Schedule (subject to change):** This position will be in office as needed, based on business needs.




**Position Budgeted:** $80,000\\.00 to $90,000\\.00  

**Position Summary:**  



The Development Director, Haiti Development Institute will provide the leadership and direction for all Haiti Development Institute (HDI) fundraising, communications, and philanthropic programs and related activities. They are responsible for developing and implementing, along with the Executive Director, the organization’s fundraising strategy. This position supports development for all five pillars of HDI’s work while building the systems, impact narrative, and partnerships that will sustain our work. The Development Director, HDI will develop and manage a growing philanthropic portfolio for HDI, capture and communicate impact, and support the HDI Board. They are responsible for developing, reinforcing, and growing HDI’s brand awareness and reputation through consistent communications and donor stewardship. In addition, they will aim to increase the number of funders, overall funding, and ultimately the impact of philanthropic capital to Haiti, which includes organizing our biannual Haiti Funders Conferences. They will also oversee HDI’s bourgeoning philanthropic consulting and intermediary services for philanthropists and philanthropic institutions.



**Essential Functions:**


* **Expanding Fundraising Capacity to Support the Executive Director in Fundraising/Development**:
* + Design and execute a fundraising strategy to meet or exceed the annual budget and a multi\\-million\\-dollar campaign goal (e.g., a Campaign for Haiti), inclusive of Kolektif Arcadins, a large\\-scale collective impact initiative for which HDI serves as backbone organization;
	+ Manage the annual process to define development goals and strategies;
	+ Working closely with relevant staff and consultants, develop and manage regular fundraising appeals and campaigns;
	+ Provide transparent and timely reporting on performance to funders and partners;
	+ Create and manage processes for alignment of program, fundraising, and outreach activities to help ensure consistency in purpose, priorities, audience engagement, and messaging. Translate organizational priorities into compelling cases for support across donor types;
	+ Advise on organizational strategy, positioning, and partnerships as a member of the senior leadership team;
	+ Maintain ongoing relations with the HDI Board and Kolektif Arcadins' Advisory Council; and
	+ Support the Board’s Fundraising and Marketing Committee.
* **Building Donor Relationships**
* + Serve as chief relationship manager for the HDI portfolio of funders and prospects. Play a leading role in donor stewardship and cultivation and maintain ongoing relationships with major donors;
	+ Identify, cultivate, and solicit new donors;
	+ Work closely with The Boston Foundation staff to ensure acknowledgment letters for contributions are accurate and sent out in a timely manner; draft personalized messages for donors as needed;
	+ Leverage the organization’s donor network to spark new connections;
	+ Define a new strategy to engage individual donors. Tailor engagement strategies for high\\-net\\-worth individuals, institutional funders, and local philanthropists;
	+ Responsible for conducting the full range of activities required to prepare, submit, and manage grant proposals to foundations, multilateral agencies, and corporate donors;
	+ Seek relevant input from finance and program staff for proposals; and
	+ Perform prospect research on foundations and corporations to evaluate prospects for potential grants.
* **Strengthening Fundraising Systems and Infrastructure:**
* + Create an institutional structure for a year\\-over\\-year fundraising plan with realistic targets and benchmarks;
	+ Develop a concise dashboard and maintain an updated donor database;
	+ Initiate cultivation process for letters of inquiry;
	+ Use Salesforce to track data and enhance systems for donor tracking, stewardship, reporting, and analytics;
	+ Collaborate with operations and program team to develop performance dashboards and workflows; and
	+ Ensure data integrity and timely follow\\-up.

*Other Duties and Responsibilities:*


* Individuals assigned to this position may perform other duties as assigned; and
* Support the HDI Board of Directors.





**Qualifications**  



*Preparation, Knowledge, Previous Experience:*


* Master’s degree, or equivalent experience, in international development or policy (or other relevant graduate degree);
* Highly skilled professional with a minimum of 3\\-5 years’ experience in fundraising in Haiti or similar contexts; and
* Program experience is a plus.


*Skills, Abilities, Competencies:*


* Successful track record of non\\-profit fundraising, including researching and identifying prospects and writing grant applications to foundation, corporate, and public donors, and experience in cultivating individual donors;
* A strategic thinker and relationship builder with the ability to develop strong pipelines of prospective funders;
* Exceptional writing, communication, analytical, and problem\\-solving skills. Clear and concise communicator;
* Flexibility in managing institutional change and adapting to a dynamic external environment;
* Experience with Salesforce and other CRM programs preferred;
* Proficiency in French and/or Haitian Creole preferred;
* Budget management skills and ability to meet HDI’s budget goals;
* Strong team player who can manage multiple assignments and meet deadlines;
* Resourceful, entrepreneurial, and able to work independently;
* Mature interpersonal style, ability to interact well with a diverse range of people;
* Familiarity with Haitian culture and the Boston Haitian community is a plus, or willingness to learn;
* Demonstrated Proficiency in Excel and PowerPoint;
* Meticulous attention to detail with the ability to synthesize information;
* Ability to travel to remote locations in Haiti up to 20\\-30% of the time; and
* Ability to demonstrate Strong project management and administrative skills.





**Working Conditions \\& Physical Demands:**


* Ability to work for long periods of time at a workstation;
* Ability to travel to remote locations in Haiti up to 30% of the time;
* Ability to use a computer monitor and keyboard for long periods of time; and
* Ability to work onsite and remotely, as required.





